   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "notes-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- **Purpose.** Compare the Keynesian-cross shift of IS ($\\Delta G/(1 - b)$ at an unchanged rate) with the IS-LM outcome, where higher output raises money demand and the rate, crowding out investment. Then use the money supply: shift LM, hold the rate fixed against a fiscal expansion (interest-rate targeting) or offset it, and see that at the zero lower bound extra money changes nothing while fiscal policy is barely crowded out.\n- **Model.** A view of [Linear IS-LM Model](note:linear-is-lm-model); dashed curves are before the change, solid after.\n- **Elements.** Panel `islm`: curves `is0`, `lm0`, `is1`, `lm1`; points `eq0`, `eq1`, `kc`; line `kcshift`. Readouts `Y1_out`, `r1_out`, `dY_out`, `crowd_out`, `Ip_out`."
      }
     ],
     "words": 109,
     "symbols": [
      "$a$, $b$",
      "$T$, $G$",
      "$\\Delta G$, $\\Delta I^P$"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "notes-2",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- **Goal it supports.** `abs(r1 - r0) < 0.01` with $\\Delta G = 100$: met only at $\\Delta M = 250$ on the step-50 grid.\n- **Tests.** Python, closed form and root-finder."
      }
     ],
     "words": 25,
